  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 200 PM CDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nSunday night through Saturday:\n\nHigh pressure will linger over the region Sunday night through\nTuesday, leading to mostly clear skies and dry weather. Weak\nsynoptic winds expected, with localized light lake breezes over\nfar eastern WI. Clear and calm nights should allow overnight lows\nin the 50s (locally warmer by the lake), with daytime highs around\n80 Monday trending up into the low 80s Tuesday (locally cooler by\nthe lake). \n\nLow pressure develops over the central Great Plains Tuesday,\nworking with the aforementioned high pressure (departed east) to\nhold the relatively warm and somewhat humid airmass in place over\nmuch of WI (dewpoints in the 60s). Meanwhile, deep low pressure \nover Hudson Bay drags a SW to NE oriented cold front into the \nMidwest. This frontal boundary should bisect WI at some point\nTuesday PM, and though it's surface wind field and baroclinicity \nmay begin to wash out, it phases with an approaching longwave \ntrough in the upper jet, with some speed divergence overspreading \nit. As such, we could see a broken line of showers and \nthunderstorms along it, tracking southeastward into southern WI \nTuesday evening into Wednesday (overall precip chances 40-60%). \nEnsemble estimates of CAPE in advance of the front Tuesday evening\nrange anywhere from around 1,000 to 2,000 joules, though the \nmodeled positioning of the frontal boundary + QPF + wind shear \nreveal the convection may lag behind the peak instability, \nfiring in northern / central WI early evening and arriving later\nTuesday night for Southern WI. As such, there is no organized \npotential for strong to severe storms with the front at this \ntime, but we will continue to monitor. Similarly, the current \ntiming for the front and precip would leave Wednesday morning \ncloudy with rain chances. But, depending on the first wave of \nactivity, a relatively dry and cloud free morning on Wednesday \ncould allow airmass recovery for convection Wednesday afternoon \nand evening (a scenario shown in a couple ensemble members).\n\nFrom Wednesday night through late next week, the baroclinic zone \nis forecast to remain nearby, and the upper air pattern shifts\nslightly east allowing for stronger (40-50kt) bulk shear values \nto arrive. Occasional shortwave troughs and jet entrances pass by,\ngiving merit to 10-35% chances for showers and storms. The\nstronger shear could enable stronger storms, but timing of the \ntrigger mechanisms with peak diurnal instability would be required\nfor any organized severe threat. \n\nSheppard",
